1
00:04:26,666 --> 00:04:32,666
Someone has taken their love of sequels one step too far.

2
00:08:53,333 --> 00:08:59,333
Scream 2 is a horror, mystery film directed by Wes Craven.

3
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:26,000
Two years after the Woodsboro murders, Sidney Prescott acclimates to college life while
someone donning the Ghostface costume begins a new string of killings.

4
00:17:46,666 --> 00:17:52,666
Sidney Prescott, played by Neve Campbell.

5
00:22:13,333 --> 00:22:19,333
Gale Weathers, played by Courteney Cox.

6
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:46,000
Dewey Riley, played by David Arquette.

7
00:31:06,666 --> 00:31:12,666
Derek Feldman, played by Jerry O'Connell.

8
00:35:33,333 --> 00:35:39,333
Mickey Altieri, played by Timothy Olyphant.

9
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:06,000
Randy Meeks, played by Jamie Kennedy.

10
00:44:26,666 --> 00:44:32,666
Scream 2 is a 1997 American slasher film directed by Wes
Craven and written by Kevin Williamson.

11
00:48:53,333 --> 00:48:59,333
It stars David Arquette, Neve Campbell, Courteney Cox, Sarah Michelle Gellar, Jamie
Kennedy, Laurie Metcalf, Jerry O'Connell, Jada Pinkett and Liev Schreiber.

12
00:53:20,000 --> 00:53:26,000
A sequel to Scream (1996), it is the second
installment in the Scream film series.

13
00:57:46,666 --> 00:57:52,666
Scream 2 follows the character of Sidney Prescott (Campbell), and the other survivors of the Woodsboro massacre, at the
fictional Windsor College in Ohio where they are targeted by a copycat killer using the identity of Ghostface.

14
01:02:13,333 --> 01:02:19,333
Like its predecessor, Scream 2 combines the violence of the slasher genre with elements of
comedy, satire and "whodunit" mystery while satirizing the cliché of film sequels.

15
01:06:40,000 --> 01:06:46,000
Williamson provided a five-page outline for a sequel to Scream when auctioning his original
script, hoping to entice bidders with the potential of buying a franchise.

16
01:11:06,666 --> 01:11:12,666
Following a successful test screening of Scream and the film's financial and critical success, Dimension moved forward with the sequel while
Scream was still in theaters, with the principal cast all returning to star, Craven to direct and Beltrami to provide music.

17
01:15:33,333 --> 01:15:39,333
The film faced controversy following its significant issues with plot information
leaking onto the Internet, revealing the identity of the killers.

18
01:20:00,000 --> 01:20:06,000
Combined with the film's rushed schedule, the script was rewritten
often; pages were sometimes completed on the day of filming.

19
01:24:26,666 --> 01:24:32,666
Scream 2 was released on December 12, 1997, by Dimension Films.

20
01:28:53,333 --> 01:28:59,333
The film earned $172.4 million at the box office, and
received positive reviews from critics.

21
01:33:20,000 --> 01:33:26,000
Beltrami received positive critical reception for his score for evolving the musical themes of the characters created in Scream, although
some critics claimed that the most memorable pieces from the film were created by composers Danny Elfman and Hans Zimmer.

22
01:37:46,666 --> 01:37:52,666
Elfman's contribution had been specially written for the film, but Zimmer's score for Broken
Arrow (1996) was controversially used in the film, replacing Beltrami's own work.

23
01:42:13,333 --> 01:42:19,333
The film's soundtrack achieved moderate sales success,
reaching number 50 on the Billboard 200.

24
01:46:40,000 --> 01:46:46,000
A sequel, Scream 3, was released on February 3, 2000.

25
01:51:06,666 --> 01:51:12,666
The film explores themes of mask, college, campus, sequel, slaughter.

26
01:55:33,333 --> 01:55:39,333
The tone is dark and mind_bending.
